<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">An Lushan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">703–<a href="/years/757">757 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lu">Lushan</a> was a general of Sogdian and Turkic descent who commanded powerful frontier armies in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>. Favoured at the court of Emperor Xuanzong, he accumulated control of several military commands. In 755 he launched a rebellion and proclaimed his own dynasty, and he was killed by his own son in 757.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/an-lushan.jpg" alt="An Lushan" width="316" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 书格 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B8%9D%E9%89%B4%E5%9B%BE%E8%AF%B4.Recueil_Historique_des_Principaux_Traits_de_la_Vie_des_Empereurs_Chinois.95%E5%B8%A7%E5%9B%BE.%E5%BD%A9%E7%BB%98%E5%86%8C%E9%A1%B5.%E7%BA%A618%E4%B8%96%E7%BA%AA_(page_19_%E5%AE%A0%E5%B9%B8%E7%95%AA%E5%B0%86)_(%E5%AE%89%E7%A6%84%E5%B1%B1)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lu">Lushan</a> was born in 703. His mother was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürk</a> of the Ashide clan who served as a sorceress. Sources conflict about the origin of his biological father; he took the surname An from his stepfather, the Turkic general An Yanyan, who fled to the Tang during a disturbance among the Göktürk tribe in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiyuan-era">Kaiyuan era</a>. He settled in Ying Prefecture, roughly modern Chaoyang, and is said to have known six non-Chinese languages and to have worked as an interpreter in the frontier markets where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> was traded for horses. In 732 the general Zhang Shougui spared him from caning for stealing sheep and took him into his service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>An rose under Zhang Shougui. In 736 he was defeated by the Khitan and Xi after disobeying orders, and Emperor Xuanzong spared him over the objection of the chancellor Zhang Jiuling, who thought he looked like a rebel. By 742 he was military governor of Pinglu, and in 744 he also received Fanyang; in 751 he was given Hedong as well. He won favour by visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and offering gifts, and he became an adoptive son of the consort Yang Guifei. Xuanzong awarded him an iron certificate in 748 and made him Prince of Dongping in 750. After the death of the chancellor Li Linfu in 753, he became a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-guozhong">Yang Guozhong</a>, who repeatedly accused him of plotting revolt.</p><p>On 16 December 755 An Lushan rebelled, claiming a secret edict to remove Yang. He crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> on 8 January 756, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, and on 5 February declared himself emperor of a new state of Yan. His forces captured Tong Pass, and Xuanzong fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. At Mawei Station the imperial guards killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-guozhong">Yang Guozhong</a> and demanded the death of Yang Guifei. Li Heng took the throne as Emperor Suzong. Yan forces entered Chang'an, but the advance was slow, and the Tang generals Guo Ziyi and Li Guangbi regrouped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As emperor An stayed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> palace. He was nearly blind, ill and extremely obese, and grew violent and paranoid. Fearing that his father favoured another son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-qingxu">An Qingxu</a> joined the eunuch Li Zhu'er in a plot, and on the night of 29 January 757 Li Zhu'er stabbed An Lushan to death. In 759 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-siming">Shi Siming</a> killed An Qingxu and buried An Lushan with the rites of a prince, giving him the posthumous name La. The rebellion he began lasted until 17 February 763. It is thought to have caused about 13 million deaths, and a fall of 36 million in the Tang census, which many scholars attribute to the breakdown of tax and census gathering.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “An Lushan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the An Lushan Rebellion (755–763) the frontier general <b>An Lushan</b> rose with his northeastern armies, captured Luoyang and Chang'an and forced Xuanzong to flee to Sichuan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tang paid for Uyghur help against <b>An Lushan</b> with 20,000 rolls of silk and honorary titles, and fixed the horse trade at 40 rolls of silk for every horse; in 762 they paid 100,000 pieces of silk to make the Uyghurs leave Luoyang, in 780 Tun Baga…</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The general <b>An Lushan</b>, son of a Sogdian father and a Turkic mother, rose to command the armies of the north-east and in 755 launched the An Lushan Rebellion, which captured both capitals before it was put down in 763 with the help of the Uyghurs.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote><b>An Lushan</b>, a Tang frontier general of Sogdian and Turkic descent, rose in revolt against his rival, the chancellor Yang Guozhong.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-16">16 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The rebel Tang general <b>An Lushan</b> declared himself emperor at Luoyang on the Lunar New Year, founding the state of Yan, and named Zhang Tongru and the surrendered Tang official Daxi Xun his chancellors.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-5">5 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>An Lushan</b>, the Tang general who had rebelled in 755 and proclaimed himself emperor of Yan, was killed in a plot involving his own son, An Qingxu.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-29">29 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.66,112.42&amp;year=738" aria-label="Luoyang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of An Lushan"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4469 4584h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4469 4584h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.66,112.42&amp;year=738">What was here in 738 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
